  "worked_solution": "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{\u2033}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 2\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-\\lambda +1] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (2\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\lambda =1.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{1/2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{1}={c}_{1}\\sqrt{x}+{c}_{2}x.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,\u21d2\\,{c}_{2}=-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{\u2032}(1)=1.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{\u2032}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2\\sqrt{x}}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{\u2032}(1)=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 2 & ={c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ 2 & ={c}_{1}-2{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =-2\\,\u21d2\\,{c}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=2x-2\\sqrt{x}.\n$$",
